            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has some excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed along the baseboards, through electrical outlets in the exterior walls, and around the connection between your walls and ceiling. There is also some air leakage around the bottom of your kitchen cabinets.



Since you are planning a siding and insulation upgrade, I recommend that you put off any major air sealing work until then as siding and insulation will reduce your air leakage. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $105&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) of semi-rigid, breathable XPS insulation.



This includes adding the same amount of insulation to the wall between your garage and your main floor.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R20 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,390&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;46.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

Since I was not able to access your attic during my site visit, the attic of your house was assumed to have 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 10 inches (R30) fiberglass batt insulation to bring the total insulation to approximately R50. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 10 inches (R30) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $375&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;12.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <exposedFloors>

The exposed floors of your home above your garage and in the front of your house appear to be 2x10 joists with an estimated 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R12) of fibreglass batt insulation between the joists and an additional 3.0 inches of rigid insulation to your exposed floor at the front of the house.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 4 inches (R12) of fibreglass batt insulation and an additional 3.0 inches of rigid insulation to your exposed floors, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $80&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$190 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</exposedFloors>
                <windows>

The two windows on the main floor of your house are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading both of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



The windows on the upper floor of your home are all triple paned with Low E coatings, and all appeared to be in good physical shape. I do not recommend replacing them at this time. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing both main floor windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $90&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.0GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <doors>

As the door between your home and your garage space does not appear to be insulated, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STAR® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your interior garage door with ENERGY STAR® qualified door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $0.0&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 178 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. 



Because your electric hot water tank is quite new and appears to be in good shape, I have modelled two separate options for you. The first option is to add an R5 insulation blanket to your current electric hot water tank.



The other option I have modelled is to replace your electric hot water tank with a propane fired instantaneous hot water tank with an energy factor of 0.92. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an R5 insulation blanket to your electric hot water heater you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $115&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing the insulation blanket from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Alternately, I have modelled an upgrade to an an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $490&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;16.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,230&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;37.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $800&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt; GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system. You would be eligible for 1/3 of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are strongly encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.31 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



